---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Your exact age, also called your chronological age, is the number of whole years, months,
      and days from your date of birth to today, or to any date you choose. This age calculator,
      also known as a birthday calculator, date of birth calculator, or chronological age calculator,
      answers "how old am I"
      precisely. It is not simply this year minus your birth year, because that only holds once
      your birthday has passed. Enter a date of birth and it shows the exact age, the same age in
      total calendar months, weeks, and days, and how many days until your next birthday.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Age Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How Age Is Measured <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Age calculation works one unit at a time, from the smallest to the largest. Subtract the day,
      then the month, then the year. If the day of the reference date is earlier in the month than
      your birth day, you borrow a month and add back the real length of the previous month, which
      is 28, 29, 30, or 31 days depending on the month. If the month then comes out negative, you
      borrow a year. Therefore the result is a calendar-correct age that respects each month's true
      length. Leap years affect an exact age too: a span that crosses several 29 Februaries holds
      more days than a flat 365 per year would suggest, which is why the count uses the real calendar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="age-at-any-date">
    <h2>Find Your Age on a Specific Past or Future Date <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Leave the second field blank to measure your age as of today, using your device clock. To find
      your age on a specific past or future date, enter it in the "Age at date" field. This is handy
      for confirming an age on a cutoff date, planning a milestone birthday, or measuring the exact
      time elapsed between any two dates, shown in years and in age in days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>For example, born 15 May 1990, measured on 8 July 2026.</strong> That is exactly 36 years, 1 month, and 23 days, which is 13,203 days.</p>
    <p><strong>Next birthday.</strong> On 8 July 2026 a 15 May birthday has already passed, so the next one is 15 May 2027, and the tool counts the days until then.</p>
    <p><strong>Day of the week.</strong> 15 May 1990 was a Tuesday, which the calculator reports alongside the age, so the "what day was I born" question is answered in the same step.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="age-calculator-vs-alternatives">
    <h2>Age Calculator vs the Alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The two usual alternatives are mental math and a spreadsheet. Mental math means subtracting the
      birth year from the current year, so the real question is exact calendar age versus a simple year
      subtraction. However, that shortcut is wrong for most of the year, because it ignores whether the
      birthday has already happened. For example, someone born in December is a full year younger than
      the naive subtraction suggests for the eleven months before their birthday.
    </p>
    <p>
      A spreadsheet does better with a function such as DATEDIF, but it needs the right arguments and
      still will not show weeks, the weekday of birth, or a birthday countdown at a glance. By contrast,
      this calculator gives all of that at once, updates as you type, and runs entirely in your browser,
      so there is nothing to set up and nothing to upload.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="totals">
    <h2>Age in Months, Weeks, and Days <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The same age also appears as totals. Total months count every completed month, total
      weeks are the whole weeks within the elapsed days, and total days are the exact count between
      the two dates. These totals are useful for infants, anniversaries, and any time a single unit
      reads more naturally than years, months, and days together.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Why totals differ from a rough estimate">
      <p>Total days are counted on the real calendar, including every leap day in the span, so they are exact rather than an average of 365.25 days per year.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Subtracting only the years">
      <p>
        This year minus the birth year is off by one whenever the birthday has not happened yet
        this year. An exact age compares the full month and day, not just the year.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Assuming every month is 30 days">
      <p>
        Months range from 28 to 31 days. Counting months and days correctly means borrowing the
        real length of the preceding month, which is exactly what this calculator does.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Leap-day birthdays">
      <p>A 29 February birthday is counted exactly for age. For the next-birthday countdown in a non-leap year, it is treated as 28 February, the common civil convention.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
